This is my first post (obviously), so I'll try to follow the format of
a few other YAAPs I've looked through. Apologies if I annoy rgrn with
excessive details of my *first-ever ascension* after three years of
playing Nethack!

When I started actually playing the game, I tried archeologists, mainly
because they were the first on the list of roles and they started with
some decent equipment. After a while I started playing on NAO, with
wizards, which led to a lot of startscumming. Every now and then I'd
get what I'd consider decent equpiment - a =oSD, Healing and Force
Bolt, a wand of digging and a few other things that made the protection
racket easier. (I've never actually successfully completed one - I've
only barely gotten to Minetown.)

After a while I found out about monks - they start out with a lot of
food, hit hard without weapons, are decent spellcasters and generally
fit my playing style. After that I mostly stopped startscumming, and
some of my monks managed to dive further into the Mines than ever
before, but I only once made it to the level with the Quest portal. (I
died soon afterward.)

About two weeks ago, with #nethack freenode's coaching and advice, I
was able to get  not only past the Rogue level, but past Medusa and
into the Castle - which, at the time, was a bit like winning the game
to me - but I stepped off of an E-square during a huge swarm, went back
upstairs to heal, and died when two rock trolls followed me up. A few
days afterward I had similar success, learned about numerous tricks
(such as alchemy), but died after someone recommended I teleport down
to the Valley of the Dead rather than find a means of levitation over
Medusa; an arch-lich killed me with a touch of death after I stepped
off of E near the backdoor of the castle.

A few days after that, I managed to get a monk through the Castle,
through Gehennom, and through even the Elemental Planes, but died when
Famine hit for over 40hp of damage while grabbing a !oFH from my BoH. I
would have #prayed, but someone in #nethack mentioned that praying in
the Astral Plane wasn't allowed. Here's a dump and a cool screenshot of
my YASD monk with four dragons, ready to assault the local Minetown
priest:

http://tweek.no-ip.org/stuff/Famine.txt
http://tweek.no-ip.org/stuff/sacred_place.png

I didn't think I'd get a good roll like that again - this monk had a
+oID, an altar at dlvl 1, a "oR in Sokoban, telepathy granted by my
god, among other things. I didn't think I'd have another chance at
getting to this level, but that didn't stop me from playing a few
games. A few days later, I come across an altar on dlvl 3, and know
that I'm going to ascend. :)

- I immediately found three bones piles - one at dlvl 5, one at dlvl 6,
and one at Minetown, two left by Blowfun, who apparently dies a lot on
NAO. I found a lot of food, an assortment of potions, spellbooks, and
rings, and - most importantly - a +4 cloak of magic resistance. My AC
before Minetown is usually 0, *maybe*, if I can find an iron helm/shoe
set that wasn't cursed. Usually I'd have to grab as many as I could,
take them to the Minetown altar to check for curses, and test each of
them for a small +1 or +2 bonus. I now had an AC of -2, without the
helm or shoes. I think I also found a few ?oEA so I was set.
Unfortunately my cat was killed early on by a falling rock trap or
something.

- I also found the two most important items anyone can have in the
early game - a +oID (in a bookstore) and a ring of conflict (probably
from one of the bones piles, but perhaps a death drop or something
too). The wine cellar was a breeze, and I completed Sokoban *without
any mistakes* for the first time - and got a BoH out of it.

- No Big Room, unfortunately, but I found Ludios easily enough, and a
swamp as well. Dove to about dlvl 23, clearing as I went, and obtained
some good stuff, protection, etc. - though all I really wanted was to
sacrifice enough to obtain 'extremely lucky' status and telepathy.
There were at least four altars from dlvl1 to Medusa, not including two
priests and Minetown, so this wasn't much of a problem. By this time I
had two magic lamps, and promptly wished for a "oReflection.

- In my last near-ascension I wasted through Ludios with only an amulet
of reflection (most of the soldiers carried death wands - as an aside,
no Yendor soldier should be trusted with a wand of *anything*), so I
thought Ludios would be cake this time. Even with my ring of conflict,
though, I was clearly outmatched, and was nearly killed by a captain
who followed me out through the portal - until I blindly 'tested' a
wand named vanish/cancellation at him. It was a wand of teleport, and I
quickly healed up and finished him off.

- After diving to about dlvl 26, I went back up (this time with more
experience, better equipment, etc.) and *wasted* everyone in Fort
Ludios. If you haven't done Ludios with a ring of conflict, you should
- it's hilarious to see Croesus be killed by his own men. I ran into a
few minor swarms when clearing Medusa and the castle, but nothing
major, especially since most of the liches couldn't really do much to
stop me with my cloak of magic resistance. I had two magic lamps, and
the WoW had one charge, so I wished for a marker, a helm of brilliance,
gauntlets of dexterity, and a pair of speed boots - a generic ascension
kit. A few ?oEA and DA later, everything was fooproofed and +4 or
better. I wasted every other wish I had in the game on the Eye of the
Aethiopica, and never got it, because I found Demonbane and Frost Brand
lying around in Medusa and the castle, respectively, and somehow Chih
Sung-tzu thought that Trollsbane was an appropriate gift for a
weaponless monk. Oh well.

- I went through the Valley of the Dead in record time, and explored a
few Gehennom levels, and decided to tame a warhorse to make use of the
polytrap I found on dlvl 26. After only a few turns I had an arch-lich
named Killingyouguys (from homestarrunner.com) - I don't care what
anyone says, they're awesome pets. They don't eat anything, so they
can't go untame unless you mistreat them or abandon them, they can wear
armor, amulets, etc., they teleport to you all the time, anything that
isn't cold-resistant is essentially dead, and after they reach Xl 37,
they can't be *touched*. He helped me out a *lot* throughout Gehennom,
reducing huge swarms to a pile of corpses in a few hundred turns.

- I thought, however, that they could use weapons. I tried enchanting
Frost Brand to +6, and rustproofing it, so that he could use it. When I
was still confused I went to the corridor in the castle to give it to
him, and somehow managed to hit him with it - breaking weaponless.
Afterwards I hit a few monsters with a pick-axe accidentally, but would
have probably been more careful if I hadn't already lost weaponless. I
never *intentionally* hit anyone with any weapon other than a
cockatrice corpse, and even then I only used it to take out a few
disenchanters in one swarm. Still not #conduct weaponless, but, close.
(I guess this game could also be considered #conduct artifactless since
I never actually *received* the Eye, but... meh.)

- After breezing through Gehennom, I decided to try to kill Master
Kaen. In my earlier near-ascension I cast Sleep at him, and somehow it
hit. He never had a chance to hit me. I didn't think I'd be able to get
away with it this time, and was a bit worried when he started doing
50hp of damage per *round* of attacks, but after he went to the
stairway to heal I managed to hit him with another bolt of Sleep on the
second try, and finished him off with ease. Afterwards I went back
(with the make-Gehennom-less-tedious Eyes of the Overworld), put on and
enchanted my robe (since I now had another source of MR), and proceeded
to take out Asmodeus, Baalzebub, Yeenoghu, and Orcus, as well as clear
their levels - Killingyouguys took down Jubilex *himself*, if I
remember correctly. I cleared to dlvl 42 and went back up to my stash
(moved to the castle; one of the best places for a big stash, IMO).

- I still didn't have telepathy yet, so I went back up to dlvl26 to zap
a few wands of create sacrifice. I noticed an umber hulk had cleared a
lot of the right half of the level, and that I could probably see newly
generated monsters more easily if the entire level was cleared - so I
proceeded to dig out *everything*, light *everything*, dig pits and
leave nothing on the ground. I wasn't intending to farm (and I never
did) - but digging the level out did make sacrifice sessions much, much
easier. I obtained a few spellbooks, and eventually telepathy by just
hanging around the dlvl26 altar. If I ran out of wands, I casted Create
Monster until one dropped a wand, or until I was swarmed. I think I
tamed one of my creations - a warhorse - and polymorphed it (using a
polytrap one level up) until it became my gray dragon, Flammie.

- After gaining telepathy, I dove past dlvl42, past Rodney's tower, and
hit bottom at dlvl50, exploring every level and killing any monsters I
came across on the way. I think Flammie died twice during this, but
someone from #nethack advised that I zap him with Turn Undead rather
than tin him. I found the VS without much trouble, and decided to stick
around the level and let my pets (and myself) train, as well as pick up
some much-needed death drops. (The only real problems with liches, IMO,
are that you can never leave them, ever, and they always freeze any
potions that might be dropped before you can grab them. Oh, and I never
used my =oConflict again after getting my first lich - the risk of a
curse attack was too great, and I didn't want them to go untame.)

- I decided to dig out dlvl50, much as I did dlvl26. No altar this
time, but this would make it easier to create (and survive) big swarms.
I went up to dlvl26 after doing that; on the way, Flammie ate a
doppelganger corpse and became an ogre or something. After a bit of
polydancing, Flammie became another arch-lich, and after fitting her
out with some of the equipment I gathered from all of the deathdrops
(like amulets of reflection, unihorns, etc.) she became almost as
powerful and as invincible as Killingyouguys. I think I picked up a
demilich (one that I #untrapped from a pit, and decided to tame, on
dlvl 2) on the way back down, and named it Frost Brand - anyway, by the
time I got back to my big room on dlvl50, I had three fully-trained,
fully-equpped arch-liches.

- Apparently they're not magic-resistant though. First I lost Flammie
to a levelport trap. I thought it was a generic teleport trap, so I
blew my whistle again, and then Killingyouguys was teleported as well.
Since they don't eat, and are impossible to retame, I rushed up to the
castle (Flammie was found on the VotD) and grabbed some cursed teleport
scrolls, and started searching the rest of the dungeon. When I finally
found Killingyouguys, he was on dlvl6 and peaceful. He had a lot of my
stuff (like an "oLS, which apparently do not work with liches, and
SDSM) and after looking through some spoilers I learned it was
technically impossible to tame him again, and someone from #nethack
mentioned that he'd probably cause problems during the ascension run,
so I decided to kill him - something I regret more than losing
weaponless. Lost Frost Brand, too, after displacing him into a pit, but
oh well.

- The main reason I was camping at altars, dlvl50, etc. (I also dug out
the dlvl3 altar) was to obtain a ring of slow digestion. I had about
five K/C rations left when I was at my castle stash, and someone
recommended that I break polyitemless to obtain one. I grabbed a ring
of slow digestion on my first zap (by now I had over 30 useless rings
and a bunch of other stuff). I also managed to get over 30 food rations
by polypiling comestibles, but I never got a marker or anything else
useful out of it. Still, the =oSD was definitely worth losing polyless
for, since I was nearly out of food.

- Most of the rest of the middle-game was spent in dlvl 50, zapping
wands of create monster, hoping for a demilich or something to create a
swarm. Most of the swarms were small and easily reduced to nothing by
my hand-to-hand attacks and my lich's frost attack, but a few swarms
were big enough to require running to the several E-squares I had by
the stairway. On my second big swarm, Demogorgon was summoned - and
even though I had Cure Sickness to 0%, I didn't know what to expect
from him. Since I was standing on E, he ran up, giving me time to kill
whatever was left. I decided to tame the demilich and name him 'I
summoned D' - not that they'd help me kill Demogorgon, but oh well.
After clearing everything out, I ran up the stairs, ran to the other
stairway, and once I saw his pink '5' on warning I immediately burned
E, like, two squares away from him. Right as I got to the stairway, he
kept poisoning me - every time I'd cast Cure Sickness, he'd do it
again. I wasted the last charge from the wand of fire I had out of my
BoH writing "Elbereh" while stunned (from his fourth attack), and I
nearly died when I took out another wand - but my blessed unihorn cured
both the stun and the sickness, and I was able to burn it after curing
myself eventually. I took off my gloves, put on a ring of hunger
(silver), and proceeded to beat Demogorgon to a pulp. He died after
only eight hits. He didn't even leave a corpse for me to tin. But the
!oGL that he left was more than enough - I managed to make about 30
potions of full healing from it, resulting in a max HP of over 500.

- After I got bored of camping around altars and around dlvl50, I
decided to kill Rodney. He casted Double Trouble right when I entered,
but that didn't stop my arch-liches from taking him out within less
than, like, five rounds, without my help. I tinned him, grabbed the
Book of the Dead, and went back to trying to summon swarms. I thought
that the 'black glows' that happen throughout the run were a result of
carrying the Amulet - it was a bit annoying, because I didn't have many
RC scrolls, and was running out of holy water trying to uncurse at
least one ring to swap levitation with. He rarely hit my BoH though. He
didn't come back that much, either, and I had plenty of death wands in
case he did.

- I had about two potions of holy water left when I decided to grab the
Amulet. Moloch's Sanctum is *really* easy when you have pets, and
easier still when you are using conflict. The only annoying monsters I
encountered were master/arch-liches, since they continually teleported
away, but they weren't that hard if I stayed near the up stairway and
kept a *lot* of holy water in my BoH at all times. Mind flayers are
easy when you have pets that can take them out, and the other summoning
monsters can't teleport, and can just be killed the usual way or zapped
with a wand of death - though I was saving most of my charges for the
Astral Plane. I grabbed the Amulet and headed to my dlvl 3 stash
without problems.

- I stayed for a while, summoning a few things, and made my god happy
before going up. I also tamed an Archon somehow; only took, like, three
tries. I wanted to stay there for a while, but couldn't think of much
to do, since my spells would expire soon and most of my books were
starting to blank, so after loading my BoH with all the artifacts,
gems, etc. I could carry, I went up to dlvl1 and to the Planes. Earth
was easy enough - Air wasn't as hard as I thought it'd be (air
elementals are easy when you have 500hp) but I was stuck in a few very
heavy swarms by at least two titans, and was brought to 40hp at one
point. (I carried in my BoH at least five !oFH, and *this time* I made
sure to keep at least one out.) My Archon was swallowed by a purple
worm (not exactly sure how that happened...) so I just decided to leave
without killing off the handful of dragons that remained.

- The Plane of Fire had a portal that was within ten squares of my
starting position, but I couldn't get to it because at least *four*
Archons were summoning the biggest swarm I've seen in this game so far.
I'm not sure how I survived it, but the Archons were both within
attacking distance, so the swarm eventually was brought to a more
manageable size. I wandered around the Plane for a while, and killed a
few fire elementals and some other things, but left before another
Archon could stick me in another swarm (I wanted to save my !oFH, etc.
for Astral). Also Rodney made an appearance in both this plane and Air,
but he's *so much* easier to deal with when he can't teleport away. He
never even had a chance to steal the Eyes; he was down after a few
hits. The Plane of Water was problematic - not because of the Kraken or
eels (in my last near-ascension, I nearly cleared this Plane - they're
easy if you just keep at least one /oTeleport out) but because my
liches were hard to keep track of, since they never teleported over the
water. I think 'I summoned D' was killed, or something, but I assumed
that it took the portal - I located it in a short amount of time and
took it to the Astral Plane.

- The last time I was here, Famine killed me, but I only had to deal
with Pestilence because the first path I took was the right one. The
other Angels, priests, etc. were hard enough - and alone took me to
about 100hp - but Pestilence was especially annoying since I had few
guaranteed ways of curing his Demogorgon-like illness attack. ('Cure
Sickness' expired a long time ago, and the spellbook blanked when I
tried to re-read.) I killed him once with a physical attack, cured his
illness attacks with whatever potions I had left, and ran to the west
High Altar - which belonged to Chih Sung-tzu, the neutral monk god.
After putting in a few credits and reading a mail scroll, I ascended to
demigod-hood for the first time, ever...

tweek, neutral male human Monk




